WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.980
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.140
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.300
I’ve made a particular statement many times on this channel.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:16.560
Light is an electromagnetic wave, a disturbance in electric and magnetic fields.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.940
Which begs the question:

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.120
Huh? What does that even mean?

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.040
Well, let’s start from the beginning.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.960
No no no, stop that!

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.600
We’ve talked about this!

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.020
Stupid AI.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.080
I mean from the beginning of our most recent series on electrodynamics.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.300
Whenever it’s possible, we treat the electric and magnetic stuff separately.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.060
If there’s a charge, there’s an electric field.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.500
If that charge is moving, there’s also a magnetic field.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.200
That’s two separate fields:

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.680
The electric field and the magnetic field.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.640
And people spent the majority of the 1800s trying to describe how they work.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.060
To the timeline!

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.260
In 1835, Gauss’s law showed us electric fields came from charges.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:01.450
In 1820, Ampere’s law showed us magnetic fields came from moving charges.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.620
Both confirming what we covered earlier.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.580
Bingo Bango! Mystery solved. Fields described.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.700
End of story.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.820
Wait a minute!

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.340
That can’t be the whole story, right?

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.580
I mean, we’ve seen other laws in this series.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:18.720
There was a Gauss’s law for magnetism too and, in 1831, we got something called Faraday’s law.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.180
That brings the equation count up to four.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.700
Each of these laws says something about at least one of these fields.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:31.140
Gauss’s law for electricity says electric fields point away from positive charges and toward negative charges.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.620
Gauss’s law for magnetism says magnetic fields always form closed loops.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:39.640
Ampere’s law says those closed loops wrap around moving charge.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.320
Faraday’s law is the odd one out on this list.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.940
It seems to connect the fields to each other.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.900
Basically, a changing magnetic field makes an electric field.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:49.940
Wait wait wait.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.100
One of the fields can make the other field?

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.580
Yeah. It’s pretty weird, isn't it?

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.060
Come to think of it, this is kind of big deal.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.760
If one field can make the other field, then
maybe charges don’t make the fields at all.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.280
Maybe they just affect them.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.480
Maybe they’re a thing all by themselves.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.880
I mean, we said light was a disturbance in electric and magnetic fields

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.680
and light is a thing all by itself.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:10.960
So it makes sense.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.340
In fact, that’s exactly how we imagine fields these days.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.960
Notice the arrows in this graphic remain in the same place as the charge moves.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.880
They’re attached to the space, not the charge.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.769
If there aren’t any charges around, we like to imagine the fields are still there

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:27.520
and just have a value of zero.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.980
We wouldn’t really get confirmation of this
though until the 1860s.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.460
Back to the timeline!

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.020
Thirty years after Faraday, James Clerk Maxwell enters the conversation.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.180
He thought to himself:

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.500
If a changing magnetic field can affect the electric field,

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.980
it would be nice if a changing electric field could affect the magnetic field.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.580
Yes, they should be able to affect each other!

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.340
So he took Ampere’s law and tacked on an extra term to make it happen.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.120
Did he just invent it out of thin air?

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.900
No, not really. I’m lying a little bit.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.020
He wasn’t so haphazard about it.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:03.180
He did imagine a conceptual model first, with a sea of tiny molecular vortices.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.600
That model was horribly wrong, but his result turned out to be correct anyway.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.120
That lucky son of a [beep].

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.980
Anyway, Faraday said a changing magnetic field gives us an electric field

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.540
and now Maxwell's said a changing electric field gives us a magnetic field.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.580
The circle is now complete.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.800
This discovery had profound consequences for our understanding of these two fields,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.700
but Maxwell kind of fell short in this department.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.400
His versions of the equations were kind of a mess.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.480
There were literally 20 of them.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.280
With some vector calculus, we can combine some to shorten the list to 8,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.620
which makes Ampere’s law a little more obvious, but Maxwell wasn’t interested in being concise.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:46.840
He was an experimentalist and wasn’t super great at deep truths.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.140
It wouldn’t be until Oliver Heaviside arrived 20 years later,

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.800
that we actually get the equations everyone knows.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.160
And, yet somehow, these are called Maxwell’s equations.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.320
History is cold-hearted monster.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.360
Anyway, the point is, we’ve described a way for these two fields to affect each other.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.780
Back to the timeline!

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.820
Maxwell thought about this connection for a few more years

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.400
then, suddenly, something occurred to him:

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.120
If a charge with a velocity gives us a magnetic field,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.740
then a charge with a changing velocity should give us a changing magnetic field.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:19.780
That’s when Maxwell saw the light!

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.520
Like, literally. He rediscovered light.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.820
We know from Faraday a changing magnetic field gives us an electric field.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.500
If that electric field is also changing, then we get a magnetic field,

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.180
a changing magnetic field.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.040
Which gives us a changing electric field,

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.880
that gives us a changing magnetic field,

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.560
that gives us a...

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:37.680
You get the point!

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.940
Maxwell had discovered electromagnetic waves and, when he calculated the speed of those waves,

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.580
he found it to be a constant

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:50.920
299,792,458 meters per second: the speed of light.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.680
And it’s not just about light.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:58.320
That’s the speed at which any change happening with the charge can be communicated to the field.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.960
There’s a delay between when the charge moves

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.140
and how the field responds at all of these points in space.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:08.020
It’s difficult to see in this graphic though because everything is happening so fast.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:12.700
We need to zoom way out and slow the speed of light way down to get a better view.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:14.920
Let’s imagine a radio transmission tower.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.760
Inside the tower, charges move up and down.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.960
Since that’s a type of acceleration, the magnetic field is changing over time.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.360
The field arrows nearby respond almost immediately,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.680
but the ones farther away don’t respond until later.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.720
The speed of light demands that there be a delay.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.860
In a way, the distant field arrows are responding to where the charge was in the past

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.920
and what it was doing back then, rather where it is and what it’s doing now.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.400
The result is a wave pattern, which we usually write using sines or cosines.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.660
We just evaluate each arrow in the field at an earlier time

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.160
depending on how far away it is from the source.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.020
That’s why we get this extra term.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.480
But, math aside, let’s just stop and appreciate something for a few seconds.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.440
This field disturbance is self-sustaining.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.840
You can stop the charge from moving and the fields will continue to do this.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.960
There’s now a disturbance in a couple fields that travels out through space.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:11.500
All because a charge once accelerated at some point in the past.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.540
And that field disturbance is what we call light.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.100
So, have you seen the light?

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.500
Let us know in the comments.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:19.240
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.600
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.960
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to be a little crazy.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.860
Several of you asked about the James Webb Space Telescope in the comments.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:35.640
Yes, JWST will be located at Earth’s L2 point when it’s finally operational.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:40.340
If it’s that far away from Earth though, we have to make sure it works perfectly before we launch.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.420
Because we can’t fix it once it’s out there.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.100
Anyway, thanks for watching!

